[Event "Lasker endgames: Take the opposition and go around"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/2k5/8/8/3K4/2P5/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/H17vt8vC"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/2k5/8/8/3K4/2P5/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E01"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Direct opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 53"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Endgames from Edward Lasker's classic chess book. The opposition: kings face to face, one square apart, and whoever moves gives way. King to c4. [%cal Yd3c4] } 1. Kc4 { Your king on c4 faces his on c6 with one square between: the opposition. It is his move, so he must give way. [%cal Gd3c4][%csl Gc4,Gc6] } (1. c4 { The pawn runs to c4 at once, and its spare step is gone before you need it. } 1... Kb7 2. Ke4 2... Kc7 { Drawn: his king reaches c7 in front of your pawn, and you have no spare pawn move left to make him leave. [%csl Rc7] }) (1. Kd4 { Also a win: king to d4 is fine, because your pawn still has its spare step for later. } 1... Kd6 { He faces you on d6. You still win, since the pawn move c3 can hand him the turn whenever you like. [%cal Bc2c3] }) 1... Kd6 { He gives way to d6. Walk round the side he left: king to b5. [%cal Yc4b5] } 2. Kb5 { Your king goes round his to b5 and now stands ahead of him, on the road your pawn will take. [%cal Gc4b5] } (2. Kb3 { Also a win, but b3 is a slow way round: your king steps backwards first. } 2... Kc7 { He comes back to c7 to block the pawn. Still a win, because your pawn keeps its spare step. [%csl Bc7] }) (2. Kb4 { Also a win: b4 heads the same way as b5, one row lower. } 2... Kc7 { He blocks on c7. You still win, but your king has one more row to climb. [%cal Bb4b5] }) (2. Kc3 { King back to c3 hands him the opposition: now he gets to face you. } 2... Kc5 3. Kb3 3... Kb5 { Drawn: his king on b5 faces yours on b3, and it is your move, so you are the one who gives way. [%csl Rb5,Rb3] }) (2. Kd4 { Also a win, going round him on the other side, through d4. } 2... Ke7 { He steps to e7. Still a win, and your king is still ahead of the pawn. [%cal Bd4d5] }) 2... Kc7 { He is back on c7 to block the road. Do not chase him: pass him the turn with one short pawn step, c2 to c3. [%cal Yc2c3] } 3. c3 { The pawn steps one square, to c3. That spare move hands him the turn, and his king has to leave c7. [%cal Gc2c3] } (3. c4 { Two squares at once, and the pawn runs ahead of your king with nobody to shield it. } 3... Kb7 4. c5 4... Kc7 5. c6 5... Kc8 { Drawn: his king shuffles between c7 and c8 in front of your pawn, and it never queens. [%csl Rc8] }) (3. Kc5 { Also a win: your king faces his from c5, and the pawn keeps its short step for later. } 3... Kb7 { He steps to b7. You still win, with c3 in hand whenever you need a spare move. [%cal Bc2c3] }) (3. Kb4 { Also a win, but stepping back to b4 costs a move for nothing. } 3... Kb6 { His king comes up to b6 while yours went backwards. Still a win, just a longer one. [%csl Bb6] }) (3. Kc4 { Also a win, but c4 walks your king back beside the pawn, and it must climb up once more. } 3... Kc6 { He is on c6, face to face with you on c4. You still win, by starting the walk over. [%csl Bc6] }) (3. Ka5 { Also a win, but a5 sends your king to the edge of the board. } 3... Kc6 { He takes c6. From a5 only one square keeps your win, so the road is narrow now. [%csl Ba5] }) (3. Ka4 { Also a win, though a4 is even further from the centre than a5. } 3... Kc6 { He sits on c6, and your king on a4 is a long way from the pawn's road. Still a win. [%csl Ba4] }) 3... Kd6 { He crosses to d6. Now the pawn moves up to c4, under your king on b5. [%cal Yc3c4] } 4. c4 { The pawn reaches c4, and your king on b5 shields its road. [%cal Gc3c4] } (4. Kc4 { Your king steps back to c4 beside the pawn and gives up the ground it won. } 4... Kc6 5. Kb3 5... Kb5 { Drawn: his king on b5 faces yours with one square between, and it is your move. [%csl Rb5] }) (4. Kb6 { Also a win: king to b6 keeps going forward, and the pawn can move later. } 4... Kd5 { He steps to d5, toward your pawn. Still a win, but a slower one than pushing to c4. [%cal Bc3c4] }) (4. Kb4 { Also a win, but b4 drops your king back a row. } 4... Kc6 { He takes c6 in front of you. Still a win, though from b4 only one square keeps it. [%csl Bc6] }) 4... Kc7 { He blocks on c7. Step in front of the pawn yourself: king to c5. [%cal Yb5c5] } 5. Kc5 { Kings on c5 and c7, one square apart, and he must move. That is the opposition, and it opens the road. Now watch. [%cal Gb5c5][%csl Gc5,Gc7][%tail] } (5. c5 { The pawn runs to c5 on its own, and his king gets in front of it. } 5... Kb7 6. c6+ 6... Kc7 { Drawn: his king sits on c7 in front of the pawn, and yours cannot push it aside. [%csl Rc7] }) (5. Kb4 { King to b4 steps away from the pawn and hands back the ground you won. } 5... Kb6 6. c5+ 6... Kc6 { Drawn: his king plants itself on c6 in front of the pawn and never leaves. [%csl Rc6] }) 5... Kb7 6. Kd6 6... Kb8 7. Kd7 7... Ka7 8. c5 8... Ka6 9. c6 9... Kb5 10. c7 10... Kc4 11. c8=Q+ { A new queen on c8, with check, and his king is far away on c4. The opposition opened the road, and your king walked the pawn up it. [%csl Gc8] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Choose a wing after taking opposition"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/4k3/2p3p1/2P3P1/4K3/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/mlOgRUIi"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/4k3/2p3p1/2P3P1/4K3/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E02"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Direct opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 60"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Both pairs of pawns are locked, and nothing can be captured yet. The whole game is about which king has to step aside first. } 1. Ke4 { King to e4: the opposition, with his king on e6. He must give way, and one king cannot guard both wings. [%cal Ge3e4][%csl Ge4,Ge6] } (1. Kd3 { King to d3 does not face his king, so he just copies you and both wings stay guarded. } 1... Kf6 2. Ke2 2... Ke7 { Drawn: his king keeps pace with yours, and neither of his pawns can be reached. [%cal Rf6e7] }) (1. Kf3 { King to f3 steps aside too, and he answers every king move with one of his own. } 1... Ke5 2. Ke3 2... Kf6 { Drawn: he steps back to f6 to guard g5, and your king cannot get through to either pawn. [%cal Re5f6][%csl Rg5] }) 1... Kd6 { He goes to d6 to guard his c pawn, and his pawn on g5 is left alone. } 2. Kf5 { Your king walks in on the other wing, and nobody guards his pawn on g5. Now watch. [%cal Ge4f5][%tail] } (2. Kd3 { King to d3 turns back from the wing he just gave you. } 2... Kd7 3. Kc3 3... Kc6 { Drawn: his king guards c5, and the door to g5 has closed behind you. [%cal Rd7c6][%csl Rc5] }) 2... Kd7 3. Kxg5 3... Ke6 4. Kg6 4... Ke5 5. g5 5... Ke6 6. Kh6 6... Ke5 7. g6 7... Kd4 8. g7 8... Kxc4 9. g8=Q+ { A new queen on g8, with check. The opposition on e4 made him pick a wing, and his king never got back from your c pawn. [%csl Gg8] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Bring the king back before pushing"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/6k1/8/7K/8/2P5/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/A2t5HfyC"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "6k1/8/7K/8/2P5/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E03"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Direct opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 51"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Your pawn on c4 is free, but your king on h6 is far from it, and his king on g8 is close. How do you win? } 1. Kg6 { King to g6: it faces his king on g8 and heads for the pawn along the sixth row. [%cal Gh6g6] } (1. c5 { The pawn runs off alone, and his king is closer to it than yours. } 1... Kf7 2. Kg5 2... Ke6 { Drawn: his king stands on e6 in front of the pawn, and yours is three squares behind. [%cal Rf7e6] }) (1. Kg5 { King to g5 drops off the sixth row, and his king slips toward the pawn. } 1... Kg7 2. Kf5 2... Kf7 { Drawn: his king keeps pace on the seventh row and gets to the pawn first. [%cal Rg7f7] }) 1... Kf8 { His king stays on the back row, waiting for yours to leave the sixth. } 2. Kf6 { King to f6: your king faces his on f8 and keeps him off the sixth row. [%csl Gf6,Gf8] } (2. c5 { Pushing now lets his king come forward to e7. } 2... Ke7 3. Kf5 3... Kd7 { Drawn: his king reaches d7 in front of the pawn, and yours is too late. [%cal Re7d7] }) (2. Kf5 { King to f5 steps off the sixth row too soon. } 2... Kf7 3. c5 3... Ke7 { Drawn: his king comes up to e7, and the pawn on c5 is his to block. [%cal Rf7e7] }) 2... Ke8 { He steps to e8. Where does your king go now? } 3. Ke6 { King to e6, facing his on e8 with one square between. That is the opposition, and he must move. [%csl Ge6,Ge8][%tail] } (3. c5 { The pawn runs before your king has shut him out. } 3... Kd7 4. Ke5 4... Kc6 { Drawn: his king reaches c6 in front of the pawn and stays there. [%cal Rd7c6] }) (3. Ke5 { King to e5 leaves the sixth row, and his king comes forward at once. } 3... Ke7 4. Kd5 4... Kd7 { Drawn: his king on d7 blocks the pawn's road, and yours cannot shift it. [%cal Re7d7] }) 3... Kd8 4. Kd6 4... Ke8 5. c5 5... Kf7 6. c6 6... Kf6 7. c7 7... Kf5 8. c8=Q+ { A new queen on c8, with check. Your king stayed on the sixth row in front of his, so he never reached the pawn. [%csl Gc8] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Choose the correct retreat"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/3k4/3P4/4K3/8/8/8/8_b_-_-_0_3"]
[White "Opponent"]
[Black "Student"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/7IyMYzDT"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/3k4/3P4/4K3/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 3"]
[ChapterId "E04"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Direct opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 50"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]

{ You are a pawn down: hold the draw. His pawn on d6 is two steps from queening, and his king is right behind it. } 3... Kd8 { King to d8, in front of the pawn. His king cannot get past while yours blocks the road. [%cal Gd7d8][%csl Gd8] } (3... Kc8 { King to c8 steps off the pawn's column, and his king walks in beside it. } 4. Ke6 4... Kd8 5. d7 5... Kc7 6. Ke7 { Lost: his king on e7 guards d8, and the pawn queens next move. [%cal Re6e7][%csl Rd8] }) (3... Ke8 { King to e8 looks close enough, but it is one column off the pawn. } 4. Ke6 4... Kd8 5. d7 5... Kc7 6. Ke7 { Lost: the wrong square cost you the opposition, and his king now escorts the pawn to d8. [%csl Rd8] }) 4. Ke6 { His king marches up to e6, ready to push yours off the queening square. } 4... Ke8 { King to e8, facing his on e6: you have the opposition, and his king cannot step to d7. [%cal Gd8e8][%csl Ge8,Ge6] } (4... Kc8 { King to c8 gives up the opposition, and his king is close enough to use it. } 5. Ke7 5... Kb7 6. d7 { Lost: the pawn is on d7 and his king on e7 covers the queening square. [%cal Rd6d7] }) 5. d7+ { The pawn steps to d7 with check, one square from queening. } 5... Kd8 { King back to d8, in front of the pawn. If his king steps to d6 it is stalemate, so he has to leave the pawn. [%cal Ge8d8][%tail] } (5... Kf8 { King to f8 leaves d8, the queening square, unguarded. } 6. Kf6 6... Kg8 7. Kg6 7... Kf8 8. d8=Q# { Mate: the pawn queened on d8, and his king on g6 boxed yours in. [%cal Rd7d8] }) 6. Kd5 6... Kxd7 { Your king takes the pawn on d7. Two bare kings: the draw is yours. [%csl Gd7] } 0-1


[Event "Lasker endgames: Win with the pawn on the sixth row"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/3k4/6P1/5K2/8/8/8/8_b_-_-_0_11"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/3k4/6P1/5K2/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 11"]
[ChapterId "E19"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Direct opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 68, the finish"]
[Verification "Lichess tablebase checked"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ You are a pawn up, with the pawn on g6 and your king on f5. His king is on d7, racing back to stop it. } 11... Ke7 { His king reaches e7, close to your pawn's road. What now? } 12. Ke5 { King to e5, facing his on e7: the opposition. He must give way, and your king gets in front of him. [%csl Ge5,Ge7] } (12. Kg5 { Also a win, but king to g5 only waits: you still need the opposition. } 12... Ke8 13. Kf5 13... Ke7 { His king is back on e7 and yours on f5, where you began. Still a win, but you went round in a circle. [%csl Be7] }) (12. g7 { The pawn runs to g7, and his king gets in front of it. } 12... Kf7 13. g8=Q+ 13... Kxg8 { Drawn: his king takes your new queen on g8, and two bare kings cannot mate. [%csl Rg8] }) (12. Ke4 { King to e4 steps back, and now he takes the opposition. } 12... Ke6 13. g7 13... Kf7 { Drawn: his king on f7 stands beside your pawn on g7, and yours is too far away to guard it. [%csl Rg7] }) (12. Kf4 { King to f4 backs away from the pawn. } 12... Kf6 13. g7 13... Kxg7 { Drawn: his king ate the pawn on g7, and you are left with a bare king. [%csl Rg7] }) (12. Kg4 { King to g4 leaves the pawn to fend for itself. } 12... Kf6 13. g7 13... Kxg7 { Drawn: the pawn on g7 falls to his king, and two lone kings cannot win. [%cal Rf6g7] }) 12... Ke8 { He steps back to e8. Your king goes forward, but to which square? } 13. Ke6 { King to e6, facing his on e8 with one square between. He has to move and give ground. [%csl Ge6,Ge8] } (13. Kf5 { Also a win, but king to f5 steps back and asks him the question later. } 13... Ke7 { His king comes up to e7. Still a win, but you must take the opposition on e5 before going on. [%csl Be7] }) (13. g7 { The pawn runs to g7 while his king is close enough to stop it. } 13... Kf7 14. g8=Q+ 14... Kxg8 { Drawn: his king eats the new queen on g8. [%csl Rg8] }) (13. Kf6 { King to f6 stands next to the pawn, and it looks natural. } 13... Kf8 14. g7+ 14... Kg8 15. Kg6 { Stalemate: his king on g8 has no legal move and is not in check. Drawn. [%csl Rg8] }) (13. Kd6 { King to d6 goes round the wrong side of him. } 13... Kf8 14. g7+ 14... Kxg7 { Drawn: his king takes the pawn on g7, and you are left with nothing. [%csl Rg7] }) 13... Kf8 { His king steps to f8, in front of the pawn's road. } 14. Kf6 { King to f6, facing his on f8: the opposition on the f column, and he must give way. Now watch. [%csl Gf6,Gf8][%tail] } (14. g7+ { The pawn checks on g7, but nobody guards it there. } 14... Kxg7 { Drawn: his king takes the pawn, and bare kings cannot win. [%csl Rg7] }) (14. Kd6 { King to d6 walks away from the pawn. } 14... Kg7 15. Ke6 15... Kxg6 { Drawn: his king eats the pawn on g6 while yours watches from e6. [%csl Rg6] }) (14. Kd7 { King to d7 leaves the pawn behind too. } 14... Kg7 15. Ke7 15... Kxg6 { Drawn: the pawn on g6 falls, and your king on e7 arrived one move late. [%cal Rg7g6] }) 14... Ke8 15. g7 15... Kd7 16. g8=Q { A new queen on g8. Your king faced his three times, on e5, e6 and f6, and each time he had to step aside. [%csl Gg8] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Take opposition from far away"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4k3/8/8/1p5p/1P5P/8/3K4/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/4dGrXtVG"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "4k3/8/8/1p5p/1P5P/8/3K4/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E05"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Distant opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 62"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Now the kings are far apart. Distant opposition: same column, an odd number of squares between them, and whoever moves gives way. King to e2. [%cal Yd2e2] } 1. Ke2 { Your king on e2 and his on e8 share the e column, with five squares between them: distant opposition. [%cal Ge2e8][%csl Ge2,Ge8] } (1. Kd3 { King to d3 leaves the e column, so the kings no longer face each other. } 1... Kd7 2. Ke3 2... Ke7 { Drawn: his king faces yours on the e column, and you never get past to his pawns. [%cal Rd7e7] }) (1. Ke3 { King to e3 stays on the e column, but four squares between the kings is an even number. } 1... Ke7 2. Kf3 2... Kf7 { Drawn: he has the opposition now, and his king blocks every road to his pawns. [%cal Re7f7] }) 1... Kd7 { He steps to d7. Follow him: king to d3, keeping the gap odd. [%cal Ye2d3] } 2. Kd3 { Kings on d3 and d7, three squares between: the distant opposition holds. [%cal Gd3d7] } (2. Ke3 { King to e3 does not follow him to the d column. } 2... Ke7 3. Kf3 3... Kf7 { Drawn: his king copies yours step for step, and you never get across to his pawns. [%cal Re7f7] }) 2... Ke7 { He steps to e7. Follow him to the e column: king to e3. [%cal Yd3e3] } 3. Ke3 { Kings on e3 and e7, three squares apart on the same column. He must move. [%cal Ge3e7] } (3. Kd4 { King to d4 steps forward instead of following him. } 3... Kd6 4. Ke4 4... Ke6 { Drawn: he takes the opposition on e6, face to face with you, and your king gets no further. [%csl Re6,Re4] }) 3... Kd6 { He steps up to d6. Meet him: king to d4, one square apart. [%cal Ye3d4] } 4. Kd4 { Kings on d4 and d6, one square apart: the opposition is now close up, and he has to step aside. [%csl Gd4,Gd6] } (4. Ke4 { King to e4 lets him face you from e6 instead. } 4... Ke6 5. Kd4 5... Kd6 { Drawn: his king on d6 faces yours on d4, and it is your turn to give way. [%csl Rd6] }) 4... Kc6 { He gives way toward the queen side. Walk through the gap: king to e5. [%cal Yd4e5] } 5. Ke5 { Your king slips past his and heads for the h pawn, while his king has to stay near b5. Now watch. [%cal Gd4e5][%tail] } (5. Kd3 { King to d3 backs away, and his king walks through instead. } 5... Kd5 6. Kc3 6... Ke4 { Lost: his king has got through to e4, and your pawn on h4 has nobody to guard it. [%csl Re4] }) (5. Ke3 { King to e3 steps back, and the gap closes. } 5... Kd5 6. Kd3 6... Ke5 { Drawn: his king answers every step, and yours never reaches the h pawn. [%csl Re5] }) (5. Ke4 { Also a win: e4 still gets your king across, one row lower. } 5... Kd6 6. Kd4 { Still a win, but from e4 only d4 held him, so your road was one square wide. [%cal Be4d4] }) 5... Kc7 6. Kf5 6... Kd6 7. Kg5 7... Ke5 8. Kxh5 { Your king takes on h5. You are a pawn up, and his king is far from your b pawn. The distant opposition opened the way. [%csl Gh5] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Do not rush toward the pawn"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/8/k1p5/2P1K2p/7P/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/qYdJRKeU"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/8/k1p5/2P1K2p/7P/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E06"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Distant opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 67"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Both kings want the other side's c pawn, and his king on a5 is closer to yours. The obvious move loses. Find the one that wins. } 1. Ke5 { King to e5: your king and his share the fifth row with three squares between, so he has to give way. Now watch. [%cal Ge4e5][%csl Ge5,Ga5][%tail] } (1. Kd5 { King to d5 goes straight for his pawn and leaves yours on c4 to its fate. } 1... Kb4 2. Ke4 2... Kxc4 { Lost: his king eats your c pawn first and wins the race that follows. [%csl Rc4] }) (1. Kf5 { King to f5 grabs at the h pawn, and your c pawn is left alone. } 1... Kb4 2. Kg5 2... Kxc4 3. Kxh4 { Lost: his c pawn has his king right beside it, and your h pawn is too slow to race it. [%cal Rb4c4] }) 1... Ka6 2. Kd5 2... Kb6 3. Kd6 3... Kb7 4. Kxc5 { His c pawn is gone, and yours on c4 is free: a clean pawn up. This lesson stops at the pawn the distant opposition won. [%csl Gc5] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Save a pawn step to gain a turn"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/3k4/1p6/1P1K4/8/2P5/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/ghkBBhiX"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/3k4/1p6/1P1K4/8/2P5/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E07"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Reserve tempi"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 57"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ A reserve tempo is a spare pawn move you keep, to hand him the turn when you need to. Use yours now: pawn to c3. [%cal Yc2c3] } 1. c3 { The pawn steps to c3. Your king still faces his, and now it is his move, so he must give way. [%cal Gc2c3][%csl Gd4,Gd6] } (1. c4 { Two squares at once, and the pawns get traded instead. } 1... bxc4 2. Kxc4 2... Kc6 { Drawn: his king faces yours from c6, and with no spare pawn move left you cannot make him budge. [%csl Rc6] }) (1. Kc3 { Also a win: king to c3 keeps the spare pawn move for later. } 1... Ke5 { He grabs e5 with his king. Still a win, and c3 is yours to play when the moment comes. [%csl Be5] }) (1. Kd3 { Also a win: d3 steps back but does not spend the pawn move. } 1... Ke5 { His king comes forward to e5. You still win, because the pawn on c2 has not moved yet. [%csl Bc2] }) (1. Ke3 { Also a win: e3 keeps the spare move, but gives his king room. } 1... Ke5 { He takes e5, right in front of your king on e3. Still a win: the c pawn's spare step is untouched. [%cal Bc2c3] }) (1. Ke4 { Also a win: king to e4 keeps the spare move in hand. } 1... Ke6 { He faces you from e6. You still win, and the pawn move c3 will be the one that makes him step aside. [%csl Be6] }) 1... Kc6 { He steps aside to c6. Walk in on the other side: king to e5. [%cal Yd4e5] } 2. Ke5 { Your king walks round toward his b pawn while the c pawn waits. Now watch. [%cal Gd4e5][%tail] } (2. Kd3 { King to d3 steps back, and the spare pawn move is already spent. } 2... Kd5 3. Ke2 3... Kd6 { Drawn: his king holds the middle, and you have no spare pawn move left to push him away. [%csl Rd6] }) (2. Ke3 { Also a win: e3 keeps your king near, and the spare move already did its job. } 2... Kd5 { He comes up to d5. Still a win, though your king has lost a row. [%csl Bd5] }) (2. Ke4 { Also a win: e4 keeps your king in the middle of the board. } 2... Kc7 { He steps back to c7. You still win, with your king ready to walk round. [%cal Be4e5] }) 2... Kb7 3. Kd5 3... Kb6 4. Kd6 4... Ka7 5. Kc5 5... Ka6 6. Kc6 6... Ka7 7. Kxb5 7... Kb7 8. Kc5 8... Kc7 9. b5 9... Kd8 10. b6 10... Kd7 11. c4 11... Ke6 12. b7 12... Ke5 13. b8=Q+ { A new queen on b8, with check. One reserve tempo, c2 to c3, made his king step aside, and yours walked round to win his b pawn. [%csl Gb8] } 1-0


