{ Your king on d6 faces his on d8, and it is your move, which is usually bad news when kings face each other. Find the win. } 1. c6 { The pawn steps to c6: that was your reserve tempo. Now it is his move, and his king must leave d8. [%cal Gc5c6] } (1. Kc6 { Also a win: king to c6 first, and the pawn keeps its step for later. } 1... Ke7 { He steps to e7. Still a win: your king on c6 guards the pawn's road to c8. [%csl Bc7,Bc8] }) 1... Kc8 { He steps to c8, the only square that keeps him near the pawn. } 2. c7 { The pawn goes to c7, guarded by your king on d6. [%cal Gc6c7] } (2. Kd5 { King to d5 steps away from the pawn. } 2... Kc7 3. Kc5 3... Kc8 { Drawn: his king sits on c8 in front of your pawn, and yours cannot push it out. [%csl Rc8] }) 2... Kb7 { He steps to b7, since c7 is your pawn's square and d7 belongs to your king. } 3. Kd7 { King to d7: it guards both the pawn and c8. Now watch it finish. [%cal Gd6d7][%tail] } (3. Ke6 { King to e6 leaves the pawn on c7 alone. } 3... Kxc7 { Drawn: his king takes your last pawn, and bare kings cannot mate. [%csl Rc7] }) 3... Ka6 4. c8=Q+ { A new queen on c8, with check. The reserve tempo, c5 to c6, is what pushed his king off d8. [%csl Gc8] } 1-0


