{ You are a pawn down: hold the draw. His king is about to step up next to his two pawns. } 1. Ke4 { His king steps to e4, facing yours on e6. What now? } 1... f5+ { Your pawn goes to f5 with check. He must answer the check, and the trade frees your king. [%cal Gf6f5] } (1... Kd6 { King to d6 steps away from his pawns. } 2. Kf5 2... Ke7 3. Kg6 { Lost: his king walks round to g6, and one of his pawns walks home. [%cal Rf5g6] }) (1... Ke7 { King to e7 backs off, and he takes the chance. } 2. Kd5 2... Kd7 3. f5 { Lost: his king on d5 holds yours off, and the f pawn has a clear road. [%cal Rf4f5] }) 2. gxf5+ { He takes on f5 with check. One pawn on each side is gone. } 2... Kf6 { King to f6, right beside his front pawn. [%cal Ge6f6] } (2... Kd6 { King to d6 turns away from the pawns. } 3. Kd4 3... Kc6 4. Ke5 { Lost: his king steps to e5 beside the pawns, and yours is on the wrong side. [%cal Rd4e5] }) 3. Kd5 { His king steps to d5, and his pawns on f5 and f4 have nobody guarding them. } 3... Kxf5 { Your king takes the pawn on f5, and the one on f4 is loose as well. Watch. [%cal Gf6f5][%tail] } (3... Kf7 { King to f7 leaves the pawn on f5 standing. } 4. Ke5 4... Ke8 5. f6 { Lost: his king escorts the f pawn forward, and yours never collected it. [%cal Rf5f6] }) 4. Kd4 4... Kxf4 { Your king takes the last pawn on f4. Two bare kings: a draw. Your pawn move with check bought the time to get there. [%csl Gf4] } 0-1


