{ An outside passed pawn is a free pawn far from the others: his king has to run to stop it and leave his own pawns. Push to a5. [%cal Ya4a5] } 2. a5 { The a pawn hits b6. If he takes, your b pawn takes back and becomes free. [%cal Ga4a5] } (2. Ke4 { Also a win: king to e4 first, and the a5 push can come next move. } 2... Kf6 { He waits on f6. Still a win, with a5 ready to make your free pawn. [%cal Ba4a5] }) (2. Ke3 { Also a win, but it gives him a free move on the king side. } 2... h5 { He hits back with h5 at once. Still a win, though his pawns get moving. [%csl Bh5] }) (2. b5 { The b pawn pushes past, and the queen side locks up. } 2... Kd6 3. Ke4 3... Kc5 { Drawn: your pawns on a4, b5 and c4 are stuck, and his king on c5 guards them all. [%csl Rc5] }) (2. c5 { The c pawn charges, and he takes it. } 2... bxc5 3. a5 3... cxb4 { Drawn: he has a passed b pawn of his own now, and your extra chances are gone. [%csl Rb4] }) (2. Ke2 { King to e2 backs away from the middle. } 2... Kd6 { His king steps to d6 toward your queen side pawns. Still a win, but a much harder one. [%csl Bd6] }) (2. Kf2 { King to f2 retreats as well. } 2... Kd6 { He uses the time to walk to d6. You still win, but your king is now far behind his. [%csl Bf2] }) (2. Kg2 { King to g2 sends your king toward the corner. } 2... Kd6 { His king comes to d6 while yours sits on g2. Still a win, only a slow one. [%csl Bg2] }) (2. Kg3 { King to g3 steps sideways and away. } 2... Kd6 { He walks to d6. Still a win for you, but the a5 push should not have waited. [%cal Ba4a5] }) 2... bxa5 { He takes on a5. Take back with the b pawn, and it becomes your free a pawn. [%cal Yb4a5] } 3. bxa5 { Your a pawn is free and far from everything. His king must chase it, and yours goes for g5. Now watch. [%cal Gb4a5][%tail] } (3. b5 { The b pawn steps past instead of taking back. } 3... a4 4. bxc6 4... Kd6 { Lost: his a pawn runs to a4, and your king on f3 cannot catch it. [%csl Ra4] }) (3. c5 { The c pawn pushes, and his a pawn is left free to run. } 3... a4 4. Ke4 4... a3 { Lost: his pawn on a3 is two steps from queening, and your king is far too slow. [%csl Ra3] }) (3. Ke4 { King to e4 skips the recapture. } 3... axb4 4. Kd4 4... Ke7 { Lost: he took your b pawn, so he is a pawn up with a passed pawn of his own. [%csl Rb4] }) 3... Kd6 4. Ke4 4... Kc5 5. Kf5 5... Kxc4 6. a6 6... Kb5 7. a7 7... Kb6 8. a8=Q { A new queen on a8. His king went chasing your outside passed pawn, and your king on f5 stands next to his g and h pawns. [%csl Ga8] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Win the race back to the other wing"]
