{ His king is about to take your pawn on h5, and yours can take his on f4. Then both kings race for the queen side pawns. } 6... Kf5 { King to f5, heading for his pawn on f4 from the side nearest the queen side. [%cal Gf6f5] } (6... Kg6 { Also a win: from g6 your king guards h5, and his pawn on f4 falls anyway. [%csl Gh5] } 7. Kh3 { His king steps back to h3. Still a win, and your king is closer to the queen side than his. [%csl Bh3] }) 7. Kxh5 { He takes your pawn on h5. } 7... Kxf4 { Your king takes on f4, and now it is nearer the queen side pawns than his. [%cal Gf5f4] } (7... Ke6 { King to e6 heads off and leaves his f pawn alive. } 8. Kg5 8... Kd5 9. Kf6 { Lost: his f pawn has his king beside it now, and it runs home. [%cal Rg5f6] }) 8. Kg6 { His king starts the long trip back to the queen side, a move behind yours. } 8... Ke5 { King to e5 heads straight for his pawns on a3 and b4. Now watch the race. [%cal Gf4e5][%tail] } (8... Ke3 { Also a win: e3 is just as quick a road to the queen side. } 9. Kf7 { His king comes to f7. You still arrive first, one step ahead. [%csl Bf7] }) (8... Ke4 { Also a win: e4 heads the same way, one row higher. } 9. Kf6 { He walks to f6. Your king is still nearer to b4 than his is to b5. [%csl Bf6] }) (8... Kf3 { Also a win: f3 goes round to the queen side just as fast. } 9. Kf7 { He steps to f7, and your king on f3 is still ahead in the race. [%csl Bf3] }) (8... Kg4 { King to g4 wanders away from the queen side. } 9. Kf7 9... Kf5 10. Ke7 { Lost: his king is now ahead in the race to the queen side pawns. [%cal Rf7e7] }) 9. Kg5 9... Kd4 10. Kf4 10... Kc3 11. Ke3 11... Kb2 12. Kd2 12... Kxa3 13. Kc3 13... Ka4 14. Kb2 14... Kxb4 { Both his pawns are gone, and yours on a6 and b5 are untouched. His bare king on b2 cannot stop two pawns with your king beside them. [%csl Ga6,Gb5] } 0-1


[Event "Lasker endgames: Give a pawn to free your a pawn"]
