{ A sacrifice means giving material away on purpose, to get something better. Here: push your b pawn to b4, with check. [%cal Yb3b4] } 1. b4+ { The b pawn gives itself up with check. If he takes, your a pawn is free. [%cal Gb3b4] } (1. Kb2 { Also a win: king to b2 keeps the sacrifice for later. } 1... Kd4 { His king heads for your king side pawn. Still a win, and b4 is still waiting. [%cal Bb3b4] }) (1. Kc2 { Also a win: c2 steps back and keeps b4 in hand. } 1... Kd5 { He steps to d5. You still win, but his king is closer to your pawn on g4 now. [%csl Bg4] }) (1. Kd2 { Also a win: king to d2 heads toward the king side first. } 1... Kd4 { He blocks you on d4. Still a win, with the b4 sacrifice kept in reserve. [%csl Bd4] }) (1. Kd3 { Also a win: d3 keeps your king in the middle. } 1... Kd5 { He faces you from d5. Still a win: the b4 sacrifice can come next move. [%cal Bb3b4] }) 1... axb4+ { He takes with check. King to b3, to win the pawn back from behind. [%cal Yc3b3] } 2. Kb3 { King to b3 goes behind his pawn on b4, ready to take it. [%cal Gc3b3] } (2. Kd3 { King to d3 goes the wrong way round his b pawn. } 2... Kd5 3. Kc2 3... Kd6 { Drawn: his b pawn stays alive, your king is tied to it, and it never reaches his g pawn. [%csl Rb4] }) 2... Kd6 { He steps back to d6. Take the pawn: king takes on b4. [%cal Yb3b4] } 3. Kxb4 { Your king takes on b4, and your a pawn is free with nothing in front of it. Now watch. [%cal Gb3b4][%tail] } (3. a5 { Also a win: the a pawn runs first, and the pawn on b4 can wait. } 3... Ke5 { He heads for your g pawn. Still a win, but taking on b4 first was simpler. [%csl Bb4] }) (3. Kc4 { Also a win: c4 stays next to the b pawn and takes it a move later. } 3... Ke5 { He steps toward g4. Still a win: his b pawn has nothing left to guard it. [%csl Bb4] }) 3... Kc6 4. Kc3 4... Kc5 5. Kd3 5... Kb4 6. Ke4 6... Kxa4 7. Kf5 7... Kb5 8. Kxg5 { He spent his king taking your a pawn on a4, and yours took his g pawn. You are a pawn up on g4, and his king is far away on b5. [%csl Gg4] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: A rook for connected passed pawns"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2K5/3P3R/2k5/3r4/6p1/7p/8/8_b_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Opponent"]
