[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 81"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ His pawn on d7 is one step from queening, and your two pawns on g4 and h3 are still far from home. } 1... Rxd7 { Your rook takes the pawn on d7. A sacrifice: he can take the rook back, but his passed pawn is gone. [%cal Gd5d7] } (1... g3 { Pushing g3 first lets his rook check you and eat your pawns. } 2. Rh6+ 2... Kc5 3. Rxh3 3... Kc4 4. Rxg3 4... Rxd7 5. Kxd7 { Lost: his rook ate both your pawns, and he is a whole rook up. [%csl Rg3] }) 2. Rxd7 { He has to take with the rook: your king on c6 covers d7, so his king cannot. [%cal Gc6d7] } 2... g3 { Both pawns stand on the third row now, and one rook cannot stop them both. [%cal Gg4g3] } (2... h2 { Also a win: the h pawn goes first, and the g pawn comes up to guard it. } 3. Rh7 3... g3 { Your pawn on g3 now guards h2. A win by the other order. [%cal Gg3h2] }) 3. Rh7 { He puts his rook behind your h pawn, ready to take it. } 3... g2 { Your pawn on g2 is guarded by the one on h3, so his rook cannot take it for free. [%cal Gg3g2] } (3... h2 { Also a win: the pawn on h2 is guarded by your pawn on g3. } 4. Rxh2 4... gxh2 { Your g pawn takes his rook on h2, and nothing can stop it reaching h1. [%cal Gg3h2] }) (3... Kd6 { King to d6 wastes the move your pawns needed. } 4. Rxh3 4... g2 5. Rg3 { Lost: his rook stops your last pawn from g3, and his king comes to help. [%csl Rg2] }) 4. Rg7 { He puts the rook behind your pawn on g2. Promote there and he takes the new queen. } 4... h2 { Both pawns are one step from queening, and his rook can only guard one of the two squares. [%cal Gh3h2][%tail] } (4... g1=Q { You promote at once, and his rook just takes her. } 5. Rxg1 5... h2 6. Rh1 { Lost: your queen is gone, and the last pawn is stuck on h2. [%csl Rh2] }) (4... Kd5 { Also a win: your king steps up, and the pawns wait. } 5. Rxg2 5... hxg2 { Your h pawn takes the rook and marches for g1. His rook is gone. [%cal Gh3g2] }) (4... Kd6 { Also a win: the king moves, and the two pawns still hold each other up. } 5. Rg3 { His rook cannot guard g1 and stop your h pawn at the same time. [%cal Bh3h2][%csl Bg1] }) (4... Kc5 { Also a win: king to c5, and the pawns stay put. } 5. Kc7 { His king starts back, far too late to reach your pawns. [%csl Bg2] }) (4... Kb6 { Also a win: your king steps to b6, and the pawns do not need it. } 5. Rg6+ { His check only moves your king along, and both pawns are still one step from queening. [%cal Bb6c5] }) (4... Kb5 { Also a win, though your king wanders off to b5. } 5. Rg5+ { He checks from g5, but his checks run out, and your pawns stay where they are. [%cal Bb5c4] }) 5. Rxg2 5... h1=Q { A new queen on h1, and his rook on g2 cannot reach her. You gave a rook to stop one pawn, and your two pawns won. [%csl Gh1] } 0-1


[Event "Lasker endgames: Deflect the king to create connected passers"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/7p/1Pk5/5p2/3PpKP1/8/8/8_b_-_-_0_10"]
[White "Opponent"]
