{ You are the one defending: a knight against his bishop, and his f pawn is one step from queening. Hold the draw. } 3. Nxf2 { Your knight takes the f pawn and gives itself up. [%cal Ge4f2] } (3. Nd6 { The knight steps away, and the f pawn queens. } 3... f1=Q+ 4. Ke4 { Lost: he has a new queen on f1, and your king is in the open. [%csl Rf1] }) 3... Kxf2 { He takes the knight with his king. His bishop on b6 lives on dark squares, and h1 is a light corner. } 4. Kd2 { King to d2, off toward h1, the corner his dark bishop can never guard. Now watch. [%cal Gd3d2][%csl Gh1][%tail] } (4. Kc2 { Also a draw: c2 heads for the same corner, h1. } 4... Ba5 { His bishop steps to a5. Still a draw: your king still reaches h1. [%csl Gh1] }) (4. Kc3 { Also a draw: c3 is another road to h1. } 4... Kf3 { His king comes to f3, but it cannot keep yours out of the corner. Still a draw. [%csl Gh1] }) (4. Kc4 { Also a draw, though c4 is a longer walk to h1. } 4... Ke2 { His king steps to e2. Still a draw: a bishop that lives on dark squares never guards h1. [%csl Gh1] }) (4. Ke4 { Also a draw: e4 heads toward the king side first. } 4... Kg3 { His king goes to g3 for your h pawn. Still a draw, as long as your king ends on h1. [%csl Gh1] }) 4... Kg3 5. Ke1 5... Kg4 6. Kf1 6... Kxh5 7. Kg2 7... Kg5 8. Kh1 { Your king is home on h1. His bishop lives on dark squares and h1 is light, so nothing can drive you out. A fortress: drawn. [%csl Gh1][%cal Rb6g1] } 1-0
